1
00:00:03,983 --> 00:00:12,109
[SPEAKER_00]: These questions seem to revolve around a sūtra that maybe I should read out.

2
00:00:12,629 --> 00:00:25,739
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's the sūtra, the Bhikkhuni Samhita, from the nun Bhajira, and talk about the chariot.

3
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:28,521
[SPEAKER_00]: So... Go for it.

4
00:00:29,282 --> 00:00:30,963
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know if it is enough.

5
00:00:34,610 --> 00:00:41,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Mara, the evil one, says, by whom is this creature formed?

6
00:00:41,853 --> 00:00:43,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Who is the creature's maker?

7
00:00:43,834 --> 00:00:45,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Who is the risen creature?

8
00:00:46,835 --> 00:00:48,676
[SPEAKER_00]: Who is the creature that ceases?

9
00:00:49,957 --> 00:00:55,139
[SPEAKER_00]: And Vajra, the nun, says, why do you refer to the creature, Mara?

10
00:00:55,679 --> 00:00:57,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you involved in wrong view?

11
00:00:58,512 --> 00:01:01,775
[SPEAKER_00]: This is a pile of pure determinations.

12
00:01:01,815 --> 00:01:04,577
[SPEAKER_00]: There is here no creature to be found.

13
00:01:05,498 --> 00:01:14,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Just as for an assemblage of parts there is the term a chariot, so when there are the aggregates, convention says a creature.

14
00:01:15,106 --> 00:01:19,790
[SPEAKER_00]: It is merely suffering that comes into being, suffering that stands and disappears.

15
00:01:20,751 --> 00:01:25,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing apart from suffering comes into being, nothing other than suffering ceases.

16
00:01:28,476 --> 00:01:28,856
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it?

17
00:01:29,096 --> 00:01:29,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

18
00:01:29,456 --> 00:01:30,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's quite good.

19
00:01:31,957 --> 00:01:33,018
[SPEAKER_01]: See, that's the whole point there.

20
00:01:33,818 --> 00:01:35,338
[SPEAKER_01]: The designation of a chariot.

21
00:01:37,119 --> 00:01:39,720
[SPEAKER_01]: As she says, can I have that, this one, please?

22
00:01:42,061 --> 00:01:50,364
[SPEAKER_01]: She never... she does not deny the name, the film, given to the assemblage of parts.

23
00:01:51,344 --> 00:01:51,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

24
00:01:51,884 --> 00:01:53,084
[SPEAKER_01]: There is an assemblage of parts.

25
00:01:53,245 --> 00:01:54,345
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a name chariot.

26
00:01:55,428 --> 00:01:57,029
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a functional assemblage of parts.

27
00:01:57,289 --> 00:01:58,230
[SPEAKER_01]: It's called chariot.

28
00:01:59,230 --> 00:01:59,951
[SPEAKER_01]: All of it is real.

29
00:02:00,671 --> 00:02:01,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Assemblage of parts, yeah.

30
00:02:02,072 --> 00:02:04,654
[SPEAKER_01]: You can break the chariot into parts.

31
00:02:05,995 --> 00:02:08,256
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, you can discern the parts within the chariot.

32
00:02:08,296 --> 00:02:11,698
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you can discern more particular things within the general things.

33
00:02:13,479 --> 00:02:15,741
[SPEAKER_01]: Ultimately, the most general being the five aggregates.

34
00:02:16,261 --> 00:02:20,884
[SPEAKER_01]: The nature of form, feeling, perception, determinations, consciousness.

35
00:02:21,744 --> 00:02:24,286
[SPEAKER_01]: Within that general phenomenon,

36
00:02:24,763 --> 00:02:28,284
[SPEAKER_01]: of those aggregates, you can have more particular manifestations of it.

37
00:02:29,204 --> 00:02:33,666
[SPEAKER_01]: And the experience as a whole of all of it, you can call a person, you can call whatever.

38
00:02:34,926 --> 00:02:48,430
[SPEAKER_01]: But there's a difference between if you're just designating it, or if that designation stands for your self-view, for your wrong view of really assuming the priority of that designation over those parts.

39
00:02:49,732 --> 00:02:49,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

40
00:02:50,592 --> 00:03:02,936
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the problem is, like, the questions of the King... King Milinda, I think it revolves around similar, but that's like... it's somewhat subtle, but not that subtle, but again, if people don't read it carefully, they won't see it.

41
00:03:03,516 --> 00:03:05,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, by... he dismisses.

42
00:03:05,877 --> 00:03:11,858
[SPEAKER_01]: He says that by destroying parts, you destroy the name that you've given to the parts.

43
00:03:12,719 --> 00:03:17,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Or... not even that, not even you destroy, but more like you deny the reality of it.

44
00:03:18,667 --> 00:03:29,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he said, well, see, there is a chariot, but if you take a chariot and break it up, break these parts up, then there is no chariot anymore.

45
00:03:30,012 --> 00:03:30,492
[SPEAKER_01]: No, there is.

46
00:03:30,612 --> 00:03:31,753
[SPEAKER_01]: There's still parts of chariot.

47
00:03:31,873 --> 00:03:35,034
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just it's not functional anymore, so you can call it somewhere else, something else.

48
00:03:35,795 --> 00:03:48,020
[SPEAKER_01]: So you don't... like, he might have replaced a particular designation of a chariot, but he doesn't see that he did not undo the nature of designation standing for your wrong view.

49
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:55,665
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, he might have fixed... well, not even fixed, he might have replaced or affected a particular designation, but not affected the nature of designation.

50
00:03:55,945 --> 00:03:56,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

51
00:03:56,205 --> 00:04:03,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can destroy the chariot into parts, into... or even destroy the parts into completely non-functional parts.

52
00:04:03,910 --> 00:04:07,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Still, there is a designation in regard to that you would call a pile of rubbish.

53
00:04:09,132 --> 00:04:16,575
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's equally real, as the chariot was a designation for a functional assemblage of those parts, not just when they were thrown around in a pile.

54
00:04:17,455 --> 00:04:21,577
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't, like, even conceive not having a designation.

55
00:04:21,837 --> 00:04:24,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, name and matter, nāma, rūpa, are just all.

56
00:04:24,618 --> 00:04:25,399
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your experience.

57
00:04:25,419 --> 00:04:30,361
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just, oh, undo nāma and have the pure rūpa or whatever else.

58
00:04:30,381 --> 00:04:33,082
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't even think in those terms, but they do imply

59
00:04:33,716 --> 00:04:41,318
[SPEAKER_01]: that affecting designation, destroying it, denying its reality, or whatever else, somehow frees you from the view attached to it.

60
00:04:41,738 --> 00:04:48,621
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't, because you can't actually even conceive the absence of designation, because an absence of designation is another designation.

61
00:04:49,301 --> 00:04:50,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there is no designation here.

62
00:04:51,021 --> 00:04:53,042
[SPEAKER_01]: You just designated it with that.

63
00:05:00,524 --> 00:05:03,365
[SPEAKER_01]: So, Sister Vajira here, she does not

64
00:05:05,525 --> 00:05:06,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Bhikkhuni Samyukta, yeah.

65
00:05:07,826 --> 00:05:09,868
[SPEAKER_01]: She does not... yeah, Vajira Dhanan.

66
00:05:10,168 --> 00:05:11,188
[SPEAKER_01]: She does not deny.

67
00:05:11,769 --> 00:05:13,450
[SPEAKER_01]: She doesn't say this chariot is not real.

68
00:05:14,030 --> 00:05:21,415
[SPEAKER_01]: She says, this chariot, this designation exists on account of the five aggregates existing.

69
00:05:22,095 --> 00:05:26,818
[SPEAKER_01]: But she has fully understood that the five aggregates have arisen on their own.

70
00:05:28,199 --> 00:05:31,361
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot even conceive being a master of them or owning them.

71
00:05:31,421 --> 00:05:32,061
[SPEAKER_01]: They manifest.

72
00:05:32,101 --> 00:05:33,382
[SPEAKER_01]: For that reason, they will cease.

73
00:05:34,064 --> 00:05:42,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, any designation that arises in regard to that, shares the same nature, cannot even be owned, appropriated, accessed, nothing.

74
00:05:42,246 --> 00:05:44,427
[SPEAKER_01]: It just designates this while this is.

75
00:05:45,447 --> 00:05:48,188
[SPEAKER_01]: When this is not anymore, that designation is it.

76
00:05:48,208 --> 00:05:55,230
[SPEAKER_01]: But there's no question of whether it's real or not, and what's behind it, and what comes first, nor anything like that.

77
00:05:56,215 --> 00:06:03,503
[SPEAKER_01]: So, she has no need to destroy the five aggregates to prove to herself that designation is mere hollow designation or whatever else.

78
00:06:03,523 --> 00:06:03,683
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

79
00:06:03,743 --> 00:06:07,608
[SPEAKER_01]: She knows that the five aggregates have... You can't even destroy the five aggregates.

80
00:06:08,048 --> 00:06:09,049
[SPEAKER_01]: That's incomprehensible.

81
00:06:09,730 --> 00:06:10,831
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't change the order of the parts.

82
00:06:12,731 --> 00:06:26,658
[SPEAKER_01]: As Aniruddha points out in his critique of the questions of King Melinda, because you can undo chariot, but you can't do your own experience and, like, throw it on a pile and prove yourself that your designations, because of that, are not real.

83
00:06:27,259 --> 00:06:34,442
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a fantasy that doesn't even stand, like, remotely for the way things actually are, because you can't.

84
00:06:34,462 --> 00:06:39,585
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't even conceive to step outside of your experience and change it to see that it can be changed.

85
00:06:41,085 --> 00:06:43,446
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's what happens when people want to observe.

86
00:06:44,026 --> 00:06:47,607
[SPEAKER_01]: They want to perceive the change as a proof of impermanence or whatever else.

87
00:06:48,488 --> 00:06:51,609
[SPEAKER_01]: But as I said before, that's not what impermanence is.

88
00:06:51,629 --> 00:07:00,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Impermanence is in the fact that a thing, an aggregate, a phenomenon of any kind, has a reason on its own.

89
00:07:00,172 --> 00:07:01,993
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no say in its manifestation.

90
00:07:03,293 --> 00:07:05,294
[SPEAKER_01]: For that very reason, it's impermanent.

91
00:07:05,314 --> 00:07:07,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it has a reason, that's why it has to cease.

92
00:07:07,515 --> 00:07:08,155
[SPEAKER_01]: No other reason.

93
00:07:08,895 --> 00:07:10,136
[SPEAKER_01]: Just because it manifested,

94
00:07:11,399 --> 00:07:16,242
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have no access to the point of origin of that manifestation.

95
00:07:16,262 --> 00:07:20,445
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't even conceive how things are... Well, they just manifest, basically.

96
00:07:20,565 --> 00:07:22,086
[SPEAKER_01]: All you can discern is arising.

97
00:07:22,586 --> 00:07:25,088
[SPEAKER_01]: For that reason, that thing will have to cease.

98
00:07:25,909 --> 00:07:35,655
[SPEAKER_00]: But can't you also... you can... you can discern the arising, but you can also know that it is dependent, that it has arisen because of some... Well,

99
00:07:36,368 --> 00:07:37,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Dependent on something else.

100
00:07:37,969 --> 00:07:38,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Such as?

101
00:07:38,389 --> 00:07:41,630
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't arise... you can't just have designation and no rūpa.

102
00:07:42,350 --> 00:07:43,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, no, no.

103
00:07:43,331 --> 00:07:45,131
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm just talking about the experience as a whole now.

104
00:07:45,151 --> 00:07:46,292
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not just talking about...

105
00:07:46,492 --> 00:07:51,414
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not talking about designation itself here, when I was referring to anything that arises.

106
00:07:51,854 --> 00:07:54,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Because anything that arises can be taken as a thing itself.

107
00:07:55,497 --> 00:07:59,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, the Sotāpanna's insight, whatever has the nature of arising, has the nature of ceasing.

108
00:07:59,498 --> 00:08:09,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Be it the designation, be it the perception, be it this, be it that, shares the nature of arising, because it partakes in that nature, for that reason, because it manifests, that's why it's impermanent.

109
00:08:10,040 --> 00:08:14,341
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because you see it's ending, you see it's cessation, you see it's disappearance or destruction.

110
00:08:14,401 --> 00:08:14,581
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

111
00:08:15,281 --> 00:08:17,361
[SPEAKER_01]: You know that it's there, because it has a reason.

112
00:08:17,461 --> 00:08:20,062
[SPEAKER_01]: If it hasn't a reason, it could have not ceased in the future.

113
00:08:20,522 --> 00:08:23,583
[SPEAKER_01]: It could not be liable to a possibility to cease in the future

114
00:08:25,563 --> 00:08:27,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it was manifested, that's why it's gonna go.

115
00:08:28,846 --> 00:08:33,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you never see it go, you know it's manifested, thus it's impermanent.

116
00:08:34,031 --> 00:08:42,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when I say it's manifested, thus it's impermanent, because, eh... it is dependent on something.

117
00:08:42,879 --> 00:08:44,740
[SPEAKER_00]: It's also... What?

118
00:08:45,521 --> 00:08:49,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, like the five aggregates, I feel, feeling comes to be by itself.

119
00:08:52,463 --> 00:08:54,885
[SPEAKER_01]: No, they all come fine, but still, what's the dependence?

120
00:08:54,925 --> 00:08:55,986
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't see the connection there.

121
00:08:56,586 --> 00:08:58,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless... unless... go on.

122
00:08:58,648 --> 00:09:01,330
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I mean, like, if there is dependence on the other four.

123
00:09:02,971 --> 00:09:07,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, but the other fours are equally dependent then on each other.

124
00:09:07,334 --> 00:09:08,135
[SPEAKER_01]: On each other, yeah, yeah.

125
00:09:08,315 --> 00:09:21,625
[SPEAKER_01]: So, which means that you basically, from an arisen thing, that has arisen, you went into comparison with other arisen things, and now you treat them as a group, failing to see that that entire group is just one arisen thing also.

126
00:09:22,110 --> 00:09:24,470
[SPEAKER_01]: On a more general level, and that is impermanent.

127
00:09:26,251 --> 00:09:30,432
[SPEAKER_01]: For no other reason than the reason of... because it has a reason.

128
00:09:30,552 --> 00:09:35,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, you can get five audience, you can say they are... well, they depend on each other.

129
00:09:35,032 --> 00:09:37,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, on what all five of them depend then?

130
00:09:38,273 --> 00:09:38,713
[SPEAKER_01]: On nothing.

131
00:09:40,273 --> 00:09:44,074
[SPEAKER_01]: Because any dependence you look of that kind is a linear dependence that you can perceive.

132
00:09:47,855 --> 00:09:49,255
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't need to depend on anything.

133
00:09:52,920 --> 00:09:58,644
[SPEAKER_01]: Just the fact that they have manifested, and that your sense of self comes secondary to that, and always will be.

134
00:09:59,364 --> 00:10:00,045
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't exit.

135
00:10:00,285 --> 00:10:02,566
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't be the reason for the manifestation.

136
00:10:02,626 --> 00:10:03,887
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't...

137
00:10:03,907 --> 00:10:05,908
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm here, and then create them out there.

138
00:10:06,108 --> 00:10:06,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

139
00:10:06,409 --> 00:10:11,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Because even the thought of I'm here is already preceded by the appearance of the five Ascetics.

140
00:10:12,533 --> 00:10:16,395
[SPEAKER_01]: So they're always ahead of you, or below you, or in front of you, or around you.

141
00:10:16,415 --> 00:10:17,796
[SPEAKER_01]: You're enclosed within them.

142
00:10:18,316 --> 00:10:19,577
[SPEAKER_01]: That would be the most accurate way to put it.

143
00:10:23,619 --> 00:10:27,562
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sure, yeah, there is no feeling without perception and intention.

144
00:10:27,582 --> 00:10:29,324
[SPEAKER_01]: There is an intention without feeling and perception.

145
00:10:29,364 --> 00:10:42,695
[SPEAKER_01]: So, depending on what approach you take, the point is, well, all of them, as the experience as a whole, as general as you can push it, share the same nature of being manifested.

146
00:10:43,776 --> 00:10:46,078
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the sole reason for their impermanence.

147
00:10:47,979 --> 00:10:52,443
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't explain that using linear explanations, because if you could,

148
00:10:52,951 --> 00:11:02,319
[SPEAKER_01]: Means that our view could be explained, could be deduced by observing things, but you can't.

149
00:11:02,759 --> 00:11:04,160
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's so subtle to get it.

150
00:11:04,240 --> 00:11:12,267
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still a view, but it comes only personally as a direct insight, based on, obviously, external instructions, knowing what to look for.

151
00:11:14,689 --> 00:11:17,551
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's always summed up in that general, very general

152
00:11:20,133 --> 00:11:23,595
[SPEAKER_01]: Description of whatever has a nature arising, has a nature ceasing.

153
00:11:27,878 --> 00:11:30,319
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is basically the other word putting it, it's impermanent.

154
00:11:31,160 --> 00:11:33,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever has a nature arising, it's impermanent.

155
00:11:35,763 --> 00:11:36,843
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the whole point.

156
00:11:36,863 --> 00:11:39,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Your five aggregates don't cease for as long as you're alive.

157
00:11:39,405 --> 00:11:46,890
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you need them to actually observe their cessation in order to understand impermanence, that's a constriction in terms of means, because you'll be dead.

158
00:11:47,640 --> 00:11:48,881
[SPEAKER_01]: When they cease...

159
00:11:48,901 --> 00:11:50,361
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can you observe impermanence then?

160
00:11:50,722 --> 00:11:51,142
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

161
00:11:52,182 --> 00:12:00,646
[SPEAKER_01]: So, impermanence is not in the sub... in the scientific observation of... of things disintegrating.

162
00:12:00,846 --> 00:12:13,933
[SPEAKER_01]: The impermanence is in the fact that you have no control, no input in that very subtle, very general notion of things arising, manifesting.

163
00:12:21,845 --> 00:12:25,048
[SPEAKER_01]: And the experience of destruction of things, it's another arising.

164
00:12:28,691 --> 00:12:33,935
[SPEAKER_01]: So, arising is manifest, ceasing is manifest, changing whilst ascending is also manifest.

165
00:12:35,597 --> 00:12:37,778
[SPEAKER_01]: And because things manifest, they're impermanent.

166
00:12:39,039 --> 00:12:41,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because they have a duration or a shelf life.

167
00:12:48,607 --> 00:12:49,288
[SPEAKER_00]: There's always a sun.

168
00:12:50,114 --> 00:12:56,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe I can't remember far back enough, or it's... things are always changing.

169
00:12:56,839 --> 00:13:04,546
[SPEAKER_00]: There's always things just taking on different forms, never-ending, just taking on another form.

170
00:13:04,926 --> 00:13:05,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

171
00:13:05,246 --> 00:13:07,568
[SPEAKER_00]: Evolving, becoming.

172
00:13:08,789 --> 00:13:10,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I am...

173
00:13:10,130 --> 00:13:10,871
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't remember

174
00:13:12,247 --> 00:13:14,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Our previous life or previous forms...

175
00:13:14,648 --> 00:13:16,009
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's a durationless happening.

176
00:13:16,869 --> 00:13:23,213
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you fail to see, right there and then, that you have a thought that connects all of those forms.

177
00:13:24,133 --> 00:13:37,060
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're not seeing that thought as impermanent, which is where your Self is, which is why there are wrong views, which is why people can't think that, and which is why people do think, ah, like, the matter,

178
00:13:38,245 --> 00:13:42,368
[SPEAKER_01]: The four great elements of five, depending on what division you take.

179
00:13:42,668 --> 00:13:44,509
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's, like, listeners' wrong views in the suttas.

180
00:13:45,370 --> 00:13:51,554
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing really destroyed, nothing really changes, they're always there, they just change its shape or whatever else, but it's always the same thing.

181
00:13:51,574 --> 00:13:52,975
[SPEAKER_01]: It has been, always will be.

182
00:13:54,175 --> 00:14:00,339
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether you chop someone's head off or whatever, it's just elements going through elements, nothing exists, nothing happens.

183
00:14:00,399 --> 00:14:07,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Failing to see that that is a very gratuitous thought, a view that you adopted.

184
00:14:08,445 --> 00:14:10,106
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have no proof for it.

185
00:14:11,487 --> 00:14:17,912
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you can't remember what was in your previous life, how can you remember that it was there, if you can't remember that it wasn't?

186
00:14:19,553 --> 00:14:19,973
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

187
00:14:20,233 --> 00:14:23,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you decide, because it's more pleasing to you, for whatever reason.

188
00:14:23,776 --> 00:14:25,037
[SPEAKER_01]: You decide that there was.

189
00:14:26,017 --> 00:14:27,518
[SPEAKER_01]: You decide that there will be.

190
00:14:29,700 --> 00:14:38,106
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were to remove all of the bad faith, which means it takes away all of the wrong views, the only fact

191
00:14:38,392 --> 00:14:53,998
[SPEAKER_01]: The thing that you can refer to it almost as a fact, as a law, as something that is undeniable, as something that you did not create, that you did not concoct in your thoughts or assumptions, would be whatever has a reason, it's impermanent.

192
00:14:54,338 --> 00:14:54,698
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

193
00:14:55,058 --> 00:14:55,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Only that.

194
00:14:56,179 --> 00:15:00,160
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, the whole dhamma is built just on that, or within that.

195
00:15:06,362 --> 00:15:08,063
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet, you can build pretty far.

196
00:15:14,299 --> 00:15:21,040
[SPEAKER_00]: When you look back to the memories of your past, you connect it.

197
00:15:21,620 --> 00:15:22,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

198
00:15:22,080 --> 00:15:32,422
[SPEAKER_01]: You make that connection right here, right now, for which you have no proof anymore than the fact that you don't have proof whether it was really always changing or not, or impermanent.

199
00:15:32,962 --> 00:15:34,782
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know, which means you decide.

200
00:15:36,223 --> 00:15:38,643
[SPEAKER_01]: You make an assumption that it was so.

201
00:15:40,343 --> 00:15:48,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I was... like you have a photo of yourself as a child, and you, as a teenager, then you make the connection.

202
00:15:49,586 --> 00:15:51,327
[SPEAKER_00]: You look at the photo and say, that's who I was.

203
00:15:52,747 --> 00:15:53,407
[SPEAKER_00]: That's who I am.

204
00:15:53,467 --> 00:15:58,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there is a connection, in a sense that there is a connection between particular and general.

205
00:16:00,190 --> 00:16:04,311
[SPEAKER_01]: It is still the enduring body that is changing while remaining the same.

206
00:16:05,871 --> 00:16:07,352
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a one-off shoot from your mother,

207
00:16:09,860 --> 00:16:19,749
[SPEAKER_01]: That is now growing old and aging and so on, while overall remaining the same body, the same projects.

208
00:16:19,769 --> 00:16:23,092
[SPEAKER_01]: But there's just... there's just things existing on different levels of generality.

209
00:16:23,112 --> 00:16:28,797
[SPEAKER_01]: There are particular changes that are allowed to, because on a more general level, the thing remains the same.

210
00:16:29,858 --> 00:16:33,642
[SPEAKER_01]: So, arising, ceasing, and changing while standing.

211
00:16:35,460 --> 00:16:37,001
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not all linear in front of you.

212
00:16:37,241 --> 00:16:40,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, if you approach your designation in that sense, you won't see it as a designation.

213
00:16:41,982 --> 00:16:43,463
[SPEAKER_01]: It will always be in regard to that.

214
00:16:44,323 --> 00:16:45,423
[SPEAKER_01]: But none of that is a problem.

215
00:16:46,744 --> 00:16:50,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, eh... Can you, eh... Yeah.

216
00:16:50,985 --> 00:16:56,767
[SPEAKER_01]: None of that is a problem, because... things need to exist.

217
00:16:57,187 --> 00:17:01,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, things' existence is comprised on different levels of generality.

218
00:17:01,549 --> 00:17:01,769
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

219
00:17:02,578 --> 00:17:05,559
[SPEAKER_01]: A more particular change means on a more general level it's not changing.

220
00:17:06,220 --> 00:17:10,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, if you look at general level, you can see it's changing there, because on a more general level it's not changing.

221
00:17:11,982 --> 00:17:14,823
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is the ultimately not changing on the most general level?

222
00:17:15,224 --> 00:17:21,226
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the nature of form, nature of feeling, nature of perception, nature of determination, nature of consciousness.

223
00:17:21,286 --> 00:17:25,368
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the five aggregates will always remain that which is in common

224
00:17:29,232 --> 00:17:33,455
[SPEAKER_01]: So you can think, ah, so the five obligates are myself then, because that remains unchanging.

225
00:17:33,715 --> 00:17:33,895
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

226
00:17:34,216 --> 00:17:35,136
[SPEAKER_01]: That changes too.

227
00:17:36,177 --> 00:17:40,740
[SPEAKER_01]: But nature, in regard to that, remains the same, because there is a change on a more particular level.

228
00:17:49,586 --> 00:17:55,350
[SPEAKER_01]: So, Itaniel, or Craig, when he was called, when he was born,

229
00:17:56,659 --> 00:18:02,242
[SPEAKER_01]: People say that's not a... that's not the real thing.

230
00:18:02,262 --> 00:18:05,343
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just... someone's decided to...

231
00:18:05,363 --> 00:18:08,505
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why she says, why is that a problem?

232
00:18:24,616 --> 00:18:28,177
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just the aggregates and the term designating them.

233
00:18:28,617 --> 00:18:29,257
[SPEAKER_01]: There is nothing.

234
00:18:29,277 --> 00:18:30,097
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not the problem.

235
00:18:30,598 --> 00:18:39,321
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is, and it always has been, suffering being arising, suffering ceasing, suffering arising in regards with suffering.

236
00:18:39,741 --> 00:18:43,442
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the whole structure, in general, in particular, it's completely irrelevant.

237
00:18:44,239 --> 00:18:55,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I mean, it's relevant in a sense that it's confused as a source of problem, but it's not actually the source of problem, which is why it says, when there are the aggregates, you call it a creature.

238
00:18:56,676 --> 00:19:01,457
[SPEAKER_01]: It's merely suffering that comes into being, suffering that stands, and suffering that disappears.

239
00:19:02,017 --> 00:19:02,797
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the issue.

240
00:19:03,177 --> 00:19:06,418
[SPEAKER_01]: There has always been an issue, and you fix that, you fixed all of the issues.

241
00:19:06,818 --> 00:19:07,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

242
00:19:08,178 --> 00:19:14,700
[SPEAKER_01]: So, denying the reality of designation, saying, well, this is just an empty convention, as such, it is real.

243
00:19:15,080 --> 00:19:16,160
[SPEAKER_01]: What's... what's the problem?

244
00:19:16,700 --> 00:19:18,521
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's empty or full, what's the problem?

245
00:19:19,081 --> 00:19:25,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Saying that it's an empty convention means you just designated further the original convention or designation.

246
00:19:26,586 --> 00:19:30,067
[SPEAKER_01]: You designate a creature and now it's an empty designation of a creature.

247
00:19:30,567 --> 00:19:33,727
[SPEAKER_01]: You can... and you can go indefinitely like that, infinitely.

248
00:19:35,108 --> 00:19:36,688
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just the same thing.

249
00:19:36,748 --> 00:19:38,448
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still a designation.

250
00:19:39,248 --> 00:19:40,129
[SPEAKER_00]: Because people can say...

251
00:19:40,189 --> 00:19:41,049
[SPEAKER_00]: It was never an issue.

252
00:19:41,409 --> 00:19:42,889
[SPEAKER_00]: Things like, uh, I'm...

253
00:19:43,869 --> 00:19:46,010
[SPEAKER_00]: I was African, now I'm British.

254
00:19:46,150 --> 00:19:46,390
[SPEAKER_00]: See?

255
00:19:47,330 --> 00:19:48,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Designations aren't real.

256
00:19:48,450 --> 00:19:49,550
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

257
00:19:50,110 --> 00:19:53,191
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is basically all revolves around denying their suffering.

258
00:19:54,379 --> 00:20:01,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you deny the reality of things that are apparently causing you to suffer, you feel like you dealt with your suffering.

259
00:20:02,664 --> 00:20:04,365
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's a... it's a... well, it's a wrong view.

260
00:20:04,385 --> 00:20:11,569
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a big mistake from the start, because neither designations nor anything external was ever the reason for your suffering.

261
00:20:11,669 --> 00:20:14,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if it were, freedom from it would not be possible.

262
00:20:14,330 --> 00:20:14,550
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

263
00:20:14,570 --> 00:20:20,974
[SPEAKER_01]: Because those things arise of their own, which means suffering would always be manifested on its own, which means you have no say in it.

264
00:20:21,974 --> 00:20:23,015
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not the case.

265
00:20:24,136 --> 00:20:38,089
[SPEAKER_01]: So, be it a subtle designation, a coarse designation, an empty designation, a full designation, be it a real thing or an unreal thing, all of those are manifest phenomena, and as such, they're external, which means they're never the reason for suffering.

266
00:20:38,749 --> 00:20:48,358
[SPEAKER_01]: It's craving in regard to them, in regard to what you feel, in regard to how you perceive things and so on, that the suffering is there.

267
00:20:49,662 --> 00:20:52,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of craving means presence of suffering.

268
00:20:52,323 --> 00:20:59,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Absence of craving is absence of suffering, leaving the whole domain of designations, five aggregates, intact, in that sense.

269
00:21:01,147 --> 00:21:16,954
[SPEAKER_01]: But because you don't discern that, because a person is patujana, does not see the origin of suffering as a noble truth, he assumes, automatically, and that's what every patujana has in common, you can't do this by accident,

270
00:21:18,639 --> 00:21:19,820
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't do it wrongly by accident.

271
00:21:21,261 --> 00:21:28,426
[SPEAKER_01]: Every putujana assumes that when he suffers in regard to something, it's that something that's the reason for his suffering.

272
00:21:30,988 --> 00:21:32,749
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you can you can sort of

273
00:21:33,701 --> 00:21:40,428
[SPEAKER_01]: On a most... on the crudest level, you'll hate people, or you said something, he's a bad person, because I'm suffering when that comes.

274
00:21:40,928 --> 00:21:44,131
[SPEAKER_01]: It's utterly, 100% his fault that I suffer.

275
00:21:44,592 --> 00:21:50,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you might work through that attitude and realize, well, be that as it may, I really don't have say over other people, so you might start refining it.

276
00:21:52,912 --> 00:21:57,735
[SPEAKER_01]: But you won't be able to go further than blaming designations for suffering.

277
00:21:57,775 --> 00:22:08,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Blaming the apparent reality of things for your suffering, which is the ultimate reaching point, which is not really ultimate of any kind, of Mahayana Buddhism or any other.

278
00:22:08,701 --> 00:22:17,026
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, even the contemporary Theravāda Buddhism, which denies the reality of things through Western science, has proven that things are constantly changing.

279
00:22:17,066 --> 00:22:17,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, nothing is real.

280
00:22:17,967 --> 00:22:19,848
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, see, it's all an illusion.

281
00:22:20,728 --> 00:22:21,989
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a completely mistaken point.

282
00:22:22,632 --> 00:22:32,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Mistaken view, mistaken direction, that the only purpose of it is to allow you to keep denying things, so not to suffer immediately on account of it.

283
00:22:33,736 --> 00:22:35,977
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not freedom from suffering, it's just management.

284
00:22:36,737 --> 00:22:41,759
[SPEAKER_01]: Because in order to deny... well, the fact that you need to deny means you're affected first.

285
00:22:42,359 --> 00:22:45,780
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you need to deal with it, and denial will be a form of dealing with it.

286
00:22:47,181 --> 00:22:51,042
[SPEAKER_01]: But sotāpāna and then ārad, they don't have to act upon

287
00:22:51,465 --> 00:22:52,685
[SPEAKER_01]: The unpleasant feeling at all.

288
00:22:53,746 --> 00:22:56,666
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't need to do anything on account of unpleasant feeling.

289
00:22:56,686 --> 00:22:57,487
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a reason why.

290
00:22:57,507 --> 00:22:58,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they don't suffer.

291
00:22:59,607 --> 00:23:07,289
[SPEAKER_01]: They have pulled out the first arrow, so they're not hit by the second arrow of needing to do things when you experience pain.

292
00:23:08,330 --> 00:23:19,093
[SPEAKER_01]: So, psychotherapy, psychology, contemporary Buddhism, every other religion, are all forms of management of the suffering, because you don't know the escape from it in the first place.

293
00:23:19,973 --> 00:23:20,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus,

294
00:23:20,882 --> 00:23:25,443
[SPEAKER_01]: Your best recourse of action is to manage it.

295
00:23:25,463 --> 00:23:37,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you manage it to explain anyway, oh, it's my chemical imbalance, that's why I suffer, or, well, it's because, you know, my parents were not supportive of me when I was young, or, oh, well, no, it's because everything is not real.

296
00:23:38,207 --> 00:23:39,668
[SPEAKER_01]: All of these are mere conventions.

297
00:23:40,268 --> 00:23:41,689
[SPEAKER_01]: You think you exist, but you don't.

298
00:23:41,729 --> 00:23:43,449
[SPEAKER_01]: You think pain is real, but it's not.

299
00:23:44,629 --> 00:23:46,950
[SPEAKER_01]: All of those revolve around the same thing.

300
00:23:47,650 --> 00:23:49,351
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know the way out of this pain.

301
00:23:51,263 --> 00:23:59,549
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm just trying to justify it, trying to explain it, blaming it externally, but never really understanding it.

302
00:24:00,590 --> 00:24:04,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you were, then, yeah, parents didn't love me.

303
00:24:04,953 --> 00:24:05,554
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a fact.

304
00:24:05,954 --> 00:24:08,396
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not a fact on account of which I have to suffer.

305
00:24:10,077 --> 00:24:11,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Things are real or unreal.

306
00:24:11,318 --> 00:24:15,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if I... the fact that I can say that things are unreal, that's real.

307
00:24:16,422 --> 00:24:17,543
[SPEAKER_01]: That designation is real.

308
00:24:18,718 --> 00:24:23,860
[SPEAKER_01]: So, their lack of reality might pertain to a more particular level, if you deny that wasn't real.

309
00:24:24,380 --> 00:24:31,263
[SPEAKER_01]: But on a more general level, as a designation that you just uttered, that you were able to think of, that you were able to direct and designate, it's real.

310
00:24:32,964 --> 00:24:35,945
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a contradiction in terms of saying, oh, this is not real.

311
00:24:37,726 --> 00:24:40,327
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if it's not real, it just wouldn't be manifested at all.

312
00:24:40,727 --> 00:24:44,588
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be able to even think about it, let alone speak and point at it.

313
00:24:47,009 --> 00:24:48,190
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all of that confusion,

314
00:24:49,289 --> 00:24:55,773
[SPEAKER_01]: It comes out as a result of not knowing the way of suffering that has arisen.

315
00:24:56,153 --> 00:25:03,437
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing something arisen and then trying to make it not be what it is.

316
00:25:03,697 --> 00:25:04,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

317
00:25:05,938 --> 00:25:07,499
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're suffering in regard to it.

318
00:25:08,440 --> 00:25:14,423
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it would be as if a person is sick, terminally ill, and there is no apparent cure.

319
00:25:16,934 --> 00:25:18,915
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the end, he just starts denying his illness.

320
00:25:19,996 --> 00:25:23,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's not... this life is not real to begin with.

321
00:25:24,358 --> 00:25:33,162
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he starts dismissing it all, because it alleviates suffering to some degree, through bad faith, through ignoring, through turning a blind eye, and so on.

322
00:25:34,583 --> 00:25:36,324
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't fix the illness at all.

323
00:25:36,344 --> 00:25:37,645
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't affect it in any way.

324
00:25:38,385 --> 00:25:39,646
[SPEAKER_01]: It just makes you not look at it.

325
00:25:40,766 --> 00:25:44,428
[SPEAKER_01]: And the attitude of not looking is nothing else than an attitude of ignoring.

326
00:25:46,148 --> 00:25:47,789
[SPEAKER_01]: And ignoring is what a vijja is.

327
00:25:47,850 --> 00:25:49,151
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not...

328
00:25:49,171 --> 00:25:50,412
[SPEAKER_01]: I think I've said it many times before.

329
00:25:50,492 --> 00:26:02,502
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a metaphysical veil of ignorance that is sort of being uncovered one day, if you were lucky or if you made enough of the right mystical effort or whatever else.

330
00:26:03,603 --> 00:26:09,688
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance is act of bad faith, act of ignoring, act of not acknowledging, act of assuming.

331
00:26:10,429 --> 00:26:12,871
[SPEAKER_01]: All of those acts maintain the act of ignorance.

332
00:26:14,117 --> 00:26:15,979
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're fully responsible for the religion.

333
00:26:16,199 --> 00:26:18,761
[SPEAKER_01]: You're maintaining it in your daily actions.

334
00:26:20,163 --> 00:26:26,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, not on a particular level, or rather, you won't find it, you won't discern it on a particular level.

335
00:26:26,409 --> 00:26:27,590
[SPEAKER_01]: It's there, but you won't discern it.

336
00:26:27,610 --> 00:26:30,433
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have to discern it on a more general level, which is where it arises.

337
00:26:31,974 --> 00:26:34,957
[SPEAKER_01]: And general level being in regard to the nature of things.

338
00:26:39,345 --> 00:26:40,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can say this is not real.

339
00:26:40,806 --> 00:26:43,487
[SPEAKER_01]: The nature of that statement, the nature of that experience is real.

340
00:26:44,007 --> 00:26:45,768
[SPEAKER_01]: It's manifested as such.

341
00:26:47,729 --> 00:26:50,310
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why all these things need to be allowed to arise.

342
00:26:50,810 --> 00:26:51,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

343
00:26:51,090 --> 00:26:53,191
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's where the problem is.

344
00:26:53,231 --> 00:26:53,972
[SPEAKER_01]: In their arising.

345
00:26:54,712 --> 00:26:55,832
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why they're impermanent.

346
00:26:56,313 --> 00:26:58,173
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is you not seeing that impermanence.

347
00:26:59,254 --> 00:27:01,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you end up, you know, putting everything else on the top.

348
00:27:02,075 --> 00:27:05,937
[SPEAKER_01]: All you have to do is to start discerning impermanence within the thing, in the heart of things.

349
00:27:06,277 --> 00:27:08,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they have arisen, not because they're ceasing.

350
00:27:10,280 --> 00:27:22,785
[SPEAKER_01]: And the whole thing will sort of, like, you know, like in the cartoons, when you see a knitted jumper, and then a cat just pulls one string, and the whole thing just gets undone, eventually.

351
00:27:23,726 --> 00:27:25,286
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's exactly the point.

352
00:27:25,567 --> 00:27:33,250
[SPEAKER_01]: If you get the right one, and you stick to the right one, stick to the right view, it will unravel everything else.

353
00:27:51,920 --> 00:27:52,801
[SPEAKER_01]: Chant.

354
00:27:52,961 --> 00:27:53,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Something you want?

355
00:27:54,022 --> 00:27:58,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's go.

356
00:27:58,465 --> 00:28:02,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, well...

357
00:28:10,116 --> 00:28:24,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but in terms of, like, yeah, the nature of things, there arise... but if you just leave it as that, you say there is... it is impermanent, but you have to do... you have to know.

358
00:28:24,325 --> 00:28:24,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

359
00:28:24,565 --> 00:28:25,105
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to know.

360
00:28:25,225 --> 00:28:26,486
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't just naturally...

361
00:28:26,806 --> 00:28:33,631
[SPEAKER_01]: The dhamma, as the universal law, as the universal path that has always been there, can be walked, is there.

362
00:28:35,292 --> 00:28:37,353
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not to say that you're already walking it.

363
00:28:38,897 --> 00:28:41,239
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the impermanence is generally in those things.

364
00:28:41,279 --> 00:28:44,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if it weren't, you wouldn't be able to find it there.

365
00:28:44,941 --> 00:28:45,602
[SPEAKER_01]: But there's a thing.

366
00:28:46,022 --> 00:28:47,103
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to discern it.

367
00:28:47,223 --> 00:28:48,063
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to find it.

368
00:28:48,884 --> 00:28:51,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's there, that discernment is possible.

369
00:28:51,786 --> 00:28:53,647
[SPEAKER_01]: And you'll find that out when you discern it.

370
00:28:54,488 --> 00:28:58,110
[SPEAKER_01]: So, until you discern it, you have to have faith that it's discernible.

371
00:28:59,511 --> 00:29:00,632
[SPEAKER_01]: And that you're doing ignorance.

372
00:29:01,233 --> 00:29:01,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

373
00:29:01,873 --> 00:29:07,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you just say, oh, if I just be with things, that's... you're just being with it, but you're not doing anything.

374
00:29:08,203 --> 00:29:21,931
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you can't... like, that's why you need all of these, like, you know, so many suttas and and and and countless numbers of instructions and descriptions and encouragements and pointers and so on that the Buddha was going around teaching.

375
00:29:23,152 --> 00:29:23,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

376
00:29:23,352 --> 00:29:26,034
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you just stay with things, well, you're just staying with your ignorance.

377
00:29:26,294 --> 00:29:27,815
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to know what to look for.

378
00:29:29,276 --> 00:29:29,496
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

379
00:29:30,156 --> 00:29:34,999
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't keep throwing more rubbish onto the thing you're trying to discern.

380
00:29:35,520 --> 00:29:36,220
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is that.

381
00:29:36,482 --> 00:29:40,084
[SPEAKER_01]: There is just staying with the thing that has a reason, not piling up more.

382
00:29:40,624 --> 00:29:41,924
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, that's just...

383
00:29:42,064 --> 00:29:45,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, now that you're with it, now you need to start doing the work.

384
00:29:45,146 --> 00:30:02,893
[SPEAKER_01]: Now that you learn how not to keep throwing more rubbish on your experience, in terms of the wrong views and so on, you learn that, you get established in that, that's a starting point now, so you can begin a proper work of, you know, taking further.

385
00:30:09,372 --> 00:30:17,957
[SPEAKER_01]: But usually, people, you would hear, you know, Buddhist monks, satchans, whoever... That's where everything would come in it.

386
00:30:18,017 --> 00:30:20,519
[SPEAKER_01]: And every religion teaches that, in one form or the other.

387
00:30:21,538 --> 00:30:27,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Just sort of, either through denying the world or whatever, but you arrive at the point of just being with things, and that's it.

388
00:30:27,643 --> 00:30:28,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Just let it flow.

389
00:30:28,664 --> 00:30:36,851
[SPEAKER_01]: Just let it flow, just be here now, present, aware, in the present moment, just... don't judge, don't do this, don't do that, just be, just be, just be.

390
00:30:37,331 --> 00:30:39,893
[SPEAKER_01]: Failing to see that, yeah, good.

391
00:30:41,394 --> 00:30:47,840
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, that you're just being, you have to start discerning that very just being a problem.

392
00:30:48,140 --> 00:30:49,081
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the problem is.

393
00:30:49,999 --> 00:30:55,584
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else that you thought the problem was, was just extra, on top of your problem, that was preventing you to see it.

394
00:30:56,945 --> 00:31:04,731
[SPEAKER_01]: Now that you learn how to just be mindful and simplify your life, that's, like, before you begin the practice.

395
00:31:04,791 --> 00:31:05,912
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a prerequisite.

396
00:31:07,253 --> 00:31:12,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it's not to, like, not to be confused as a final goal.

397
00:31:12,698 --> 00:31:15,720
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it's so far from it, that it's just... it's still wrong.

398
00:31:16,381 --> 00:31:19,263
[SPEAKER_01]: You're still putrujana, you can still be just present with things,

399
00:31:20,027 --> 00:31:21,008
[SPEAKER_01]: We don't put it in your mind.

400
00:31:21,248 --> 00:31:22,348
[SPEAKER_01]: We don't put it in your ignorance.

401
00:31:23,509 --> 00:31:28,051
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can spend your whole life... Because ignorance won't... can't get undone by itself.

402
00:31:28,972 --> 00:31:30,232
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not there by itself.

403
00:31:30,333 --> 00:31:30,933
[SPEAKER_01]: You're doing it.

404
00:31:30,953 --> 00:31:32,774
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to learn how to stop doing it.

405
00:31:32,874 --> 00:31:33,854
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how it's undone.

406
00:31:33,974 --> 00:31:37,536
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you don't learn how to stop doing it, you'll just be... you're just being with it.

407
00:31:38,617 --> 00:31:40,378
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's the point, you know.

408
00:31:40,398 --> 00:31:41,979
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... you, you're doing it.

409
00:31:42,579 --> 00:31:43,840
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not doing itself.

410
00:31:44,000 --> 00:31:45,140
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not fast upon you.

411
00:31:45,281 --> 00:31:46,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignorance is not given to you... Yeah.

412
00:31:50,787 --> 00:31:59,231
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, again, ignorance or whatever, any other wholesome states you want to pick up from the suttas, it's all where that craving is.

413
00:31:59,371 --> 00:32:05,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, it all shares the nature of that very craving, which is something you're doing in regard to things.

414
00:32:05,735 --> 00:32:16,480
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the same sense that you can say craving is the reason for your suffering, direct reason for suffering, not that on account of which you crave, which results in suffering.

415
00:32:17,341 --> 00:32:21,784
[SPEAKER_01]: So, nothing external is the direct reason for suffering, except your craving in regard to it.

416
00:32:22,525 --> 00:32:25,967
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you can say, ignorance is the reason for suffering.

417
00:32:26,327 --> 00:32:29,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing external, but your attitude in regard to the external.

418
00:32:29,889 --> 00:32:33,812
[SPEAKER_01]: The attitude of ignoring, the attitude of turning a blind, the attitude of bad faith.

419
00:32:37,755 --> 00:32:39,696
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the reason for suffering.

420
00:32:41,797 --> 00:32:43,258
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a very distinct answer.

421
00:32:44,219 --> 00:32:45,520
[SPEAKER_00]: This couple in Norway,

422
00:32:48,937 --> 00:32:49,618
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it's in Norway.

423
00:32:51,998 --> 00:32:54,399
[SPEAKER_00]: The government said that they need to register a baby.

424
00:32:54,599 --> 00:32:55,939
[SPEAKER_00]: They need to register the baby's name.

425
00:32:56,720 --> 00:32:57,680
[SPEAKER_00]: And they refused to do so.

426
00:32:59,460 --> 00:33:00,240
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they were fined.

427
00:33:01,441 --> 00:33:04,782
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they were forced to go and register the name for the baby.

428
00:33:04,802 --> 00:33:09,643
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they called it B X J Y Z Z 1 2 3... like it's 21 letters.

429
00:33:09,663 --> 00:33:10,723
[SPEAKER_00]: I think I heard that.

430
00:33:10,763 --> 00:33:11,704
[SPEAKER_01]: I heard one.

431
00:33:11,924 --> 00:33:12,984
[SPEAKER_00]: They said, no, it's ridiculous.

432
00:33:14,507 --> 00:33:15,788
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to register properly.

433
00:33:16,248 --> 00:33:16,768
[SPEAKER_00]: It's too long.

434
00:33:16,808 --> 00:33:17,188
[SPEAKER_00]: It's too...

435
00:33:17,668 --> 00:33:18,549
[SPEAKER_00]: So, put A.

436
00:33:19,629 --> 00:33:20,590
[SPEAKER_00]: And they would find it again.

437
00:33:20,950 --> 00:33:21,250
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

438
00:33:21,510 --> 00:33:22,911
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you can't just call it A.

439
00:33:23,191 --> 00:33:23,751
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to give it...

440
00:33:24,271 --> 00:33:33,075
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they... there's a classic example that they were trying to get away from this whole institution, this whole society, because that's the problem.

441
00:33:33,095 --> 00:33:36,237
[SPEAKER_00]: That was the... they have the cause of... they saw it in them.

442
00:33:36,257 --> 00:33:38,278
[SPEAKER_00]: They think that's why they suffer.

443
00:33:38,798 --> 00:33:43,060
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they're not going to let their child go to the same... shun society, shun...

444
00:33:43,960 --> 00:33:44,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything.

445
00:33:44,880 --> 00:33:45,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

446
00:33:45,761 --> 00:33:58,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Failing to see that shunning the society is another attitude towards the society, which, in this particular case, resulted in even more hassle, for which they felt fully justified in their anger and their will, because I'm sure they thought they were right.

447
00:33:58,666 --> 00:33:58,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

448
00:33:59,566 --> 00:34:02,888
[SPEAKER_01]: They're just equally deluded, as the society that is put in the name now.

449
00:34:03,648 --> 00:34:04,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there was never the problem.

450
00:34:07,310 --> 00:34:08,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Never ever the problem was not the issue.

451
00:34:10,331 --> 00:34:12,812
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, it's the... it's a very common

452
00:34:14,442 --> 00:34:24,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether, as I said, in other schools of Buddhism, or rather all schools of Buddhism today, or other religions, that's the most you can get.

453
00:34:24,330 --> 00:34:27,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Deny the reality by one mean or the other.

454
00:34:29,434 --> 00:34:29,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

455
00:34:29,674 --> 00:34:34,478
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you've mistakenly blamed it from the start, the reason of your suffering.

456
00:34:35,138 --> 00:34:39,142
[SPEAKER_01]: And denying of it removes the apparent reason of your suffering.

457
00:34:39,882 --> 00:34:40,903
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't.

458
00:34:41,003 --> 00:34:42,324
[SPEAKER_01]: You still are suffering.

459
00:34:42,344 --> 00:34:43,325
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's just a management.

460
00:34:44,552 --> 00:34:47,534
[SPEAKER_01]: That it's a response to the present suffering.

461
00:34:47,634 --> 00:34:48,715
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not uprooting it.

462
00:34:48,855 --> 00:34:50,396
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not dealing with it.

463
00:34:51,037 --> 00:34:54,199
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not preventing it to arise in the future.

464
00:34:54,579 --> 00:34:57,722
[SPEAKER_01]: You... like, you can spend your whole life managing your suffering.

465
00:34:58,002 --> 00:35:03,986
[SPEAKER_01]: At the end of it, you still remain prey to suffering as much as you were in the beginning.

466
00:35:04,467 --> 00:35:09,130
[SPEAKER_01]: You have not affected a grain of that nature of being prey to suffering.

467
00:35:10,291 --> 00:35:11,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Being liable to suffer.

468
00:35:14,182 --> 00:35:19,585
[SPEAKER_01]: Because all you've been doing is managing the actual suffering, or the prospect of actual suffering.

469
00:35:20,386 --> 00:35:27,129
[SPEAKER_00]: It was like, you're just going to, like, just ignore as hard as you can.

470
00:35:27,910 --> 00:35:30,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the mystical absorption, yeah.

471
00:35:30,211 --> 00:35:36,655
[SPEAKER_00]: Or really enjoy the suffering, really get into it, so it's not a problem anymore, because you really want it.

472
00:35:36,755 --> 00:35:36,955
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

473
00:35:37,135 --> 00:35:38,576
[SPEAKER_00]: You force yourself to want it.

474
00:35:39,574 --> 00:35:48,939
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that is a secondary attitude, or rather a response, in regard to the fact, yeah, that it has arisen and you were afflicted by it.

475
00:35:49,900 --> 00:36:01,727
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether you accept it, reject it, both, accept it and reject it, neither accept it nor reject it, because there's nothing to accept nor reject, all of those will be a form

476
00:36:02,335 --> 00:36:09,424
[SPEAKER_01]: There are forms of response, reaction to the fact that it has arisen and it struck you.

477
00:36:11,046 --> 00:36:20,477
[SPEAKER_01]: And the fact that you have no option but to respond, but to react, means you're striking yourself a second time on account of it.

478
00:36:35,542 --> 00:37:01,763
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if one has the understanding of a certifier, that things are not one, or has that understanding of an answer, not self, then what stops him from not doing whatever he wants, whatever

479
00:37:03,380 --> 00:37:04,120
[SPEAKER_00]: You see how it's not mine?

480
00:37:04,780 --> 00:37:06,000
[SPEAKER_00]: Well...

481
00:37:06,020 --> 00:37:08,561
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he abandons responsibility towards things.

482
00:37:08,621 --> 00:37:09,341
[SPEAKER_01]: He overcomes it.

483
00:37:09,781 --> 00:37:09,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

484
00:37:10,401 --> 00:37:13,442
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's a... it's a... it's an experience of liberation.

485
00:37:15,802 --> 00:37:18,423
[SPEAKER_01]: But the burden is laid down.

486
00:37:19,403 --> 00:37:21,123
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha would compare it to a burden.

487
00:37:21,283 --> 00:37:22,123
[SPEAKER_01]: It is.

488
00:37:22,143 --> 00:37:22,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

489
00:37:22,543 --> 00:37:23,144
[SPEAKER_01]: You overcome it.

490
00:37:24,024 --> 00:37:31,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Imagine, as the suttas talk about the jhānas and similar, you were in prison, with your family left out there.

491
00:37:32,232 --> 00:37:33,833
[SPEAKER_01]: And you worry, because you feel responsible.

492
00:37:33,854 --> 00:37:41,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you're not just, like, they're your family, so you're fully throwing through, taking them as yours, but you're not in control over it.

493
00:37:42,221 --> 00:37:43,702
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're in prison and you worry.

494
00:37:45,164 --> 00:37:45,984
[SPEAKER_01]: What are they going through?

495
00:37:46,465 --> 00:37:47,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Are the bad things going to happen?

496
00:37:47,746 --> 00:37:48,406
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you going to lose them?

497
00:37:48,426 --> 00:37:49,407
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you going to be killed or raped?

498
00:37:49,487 --> 00:37:50,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Or who knows what?

499
00:37:51,029 --> 00:37:53,291
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you go out and you find them intact.

500
00:37:54,512 --> 00:37:59,196
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the relief from that burden of that responsibility that you had no input in,

501
00:38:00,356 --> 00:38:00,856
[SPEAKER_01]: You feel.

502
00:38:01,497 --> 00:38:04,059
[SPEAKER_01]: Or crossing the desert, all of those other similes.

503
00:38:04,520 --> 00:38:10,505
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it is, genuinely, feeling like you're laying out a heavy burden.

504
00:38:11,546 --> 00:38:12,927
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh... it's a good question.

505
00:38:12,967 --> 00:38:14,228
[SPEAKER_01]: What stops him from doing it?

506
00:38:14,288 --> 00:38:15,129
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing stops him.

507
00:38:16,150 --> 00:38:27,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because he... What stops him is the requirement of sīla being established first, before the dhamma takes place.

508
00:38:29,091 --> 00:38:41,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that was established, when the dhamma took place, and the self-view has been abolished and so on, he can't revert back to those things that were requiring that self-view, which was the unwholesome behaviors.

509
00:38:43,035 --> 00:38:53,479
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he sets it up, but then, exactly, he lets go of that as well, which is letting go of sīla, bhātta-paramāsa, letting go of virtue and duty.

510
00:38:54,979 --> 00:38:56,399
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's an extreme relief.

511
00:38:57,880 --> 00:38:58,020
[SPEAKER_01]: When

512
00:38:58,862 --> 00:39:01,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you lay down your duty.

513
00:39:02,386 --> 00:39:03,628
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to do anything.

514
00:39:03,938 --> 00:39:05,418
[SPEAKER_01]: You spend the rest of the night just sleeping.

515
00:39:05,778 --> 00:39:09,359
[SPEAKER_00]: But some people say to you, yeah, but what about your mother?

516
00:39:09,779 --> 00:39:12,699
[SPEAKER_00]: She's suffering and you should go and look after her.

517
00:39:12,719 --> 00:39:15,540
[SPEAKER_01]: They're still speaking out of their self-view.

518
00:39:16,400 --> 00:39:18,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Out of their sense of duty, they're still burdened by it.

519
00:39:19,101 --> 00:39:20,321
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's making me suffer.

520
00:39:20,701 --> 00:39:22,001
[SPEAKER_01]: They are suffering because of what you're doing.

521
00:39:22,101 --> 00:39:22,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

522
00:39:22,481 --> 00:39:24,361
[SPEAKER_01]: They do it because it's their personal pain.

523
00:39:24,662 --> 00:39:30,923
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, somebody can misinterpret this and say, oh, okay, so, like, you just start denying everything and say, well, nothing is real.

524
00:39:30,943 --> 00:39:31,623
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no responsibility.

525
00:39:31,683 --> 00:39:33,243
[SPEAKER_01]: I can do whatever I want, which is what people do.

526
00:39:33,917 --> 00:39:34,377
[SPEAKER_01]: But no.

527
00:39:34,857 --> 00:39:37,638
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the order is not negotiable.

528
00:39:37,818 --> 00:39:39,318
[SPEAKER_01]: The order of things.

529
00:39:39,378 --> 00:39:42,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Sīla comes first, then the dhamma can take place.

530
00:39:42,999 --> 00:39:47,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Without a sīla, whatever took place, you can be sure that it's not the dhamma.

531
00:39:50,982 --> 00:40:00,044
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and they always say that, the Buddha, when he talks in the suttas, when he would say, then in the center, he understood this, and then the knowledge has been developed,

532
00:40:01,232 --> 00:40:04,655
[SPEAKER_01]: Because his behavior has been purified beforehand.

533
00:40:07,016 --> 00:40:14,482
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever he understood then, he understood because, okay, he really made the effort to understand and his behavior has been purified beforehand.

534
00:40:14,802 --> 00:40:16,504
[SPEAKER_00]: He has a sense of shame, fear of wrongdoing.

535
00:40:16,524 --> 00:40:16,984
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

536
00:40:17,104 --> 00:40:17,584
[SPEAKER_00]: Established.

537
00:40:17,604 --> 00:40:17,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

538
00:40:18,265 --> 00:40:19,646
[SPEAKER_00]: And then...

539
00:40:20,146 --> 00:40:23,809
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sense of shame and fear of wrongdoing and the whole sīla, it's not the dhamma.

540
00:40:25,891 --> 00:40:28,653
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's a necessary ground for the dhamma to take place.

541
00:40:30,496 --> 00:40:33,358
[SPEAKER_01]: And when the dhamma does take place, you abandon that ground as well.

542
00:40:34,379 --> 00:40:37,041
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, you know, practicing dhamma, you go beyond good and beyond bad.

543
00:40:37,061 --> 00:40:39,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Does that mean that that ground gets undone?

544
00:40:39,483 --> 00:40:43,206
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, it stays as it... because there always will be a ground.

545
00:40:43,786 --> 00:40:49,951
[SPEAKER_01]: You prepared it and now you took off and that ground is left.

546
00:40:50,252 --> 00:40:50,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously,

547
00:40:51,390 --> 00:40:58,773
[SPEAKER_01]: There are different, you know, different people, different grounds, different level of preparing the ground, different habits, different so on.

548
00:40:58,993 --> 00:41:05,216
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there are many different āryas in the suttas, getting up to all sorts of relatively serious mischief.

549
00:41:06,296 --> 00:41:11,378
[SPEAKER_01]: And... which is why that sutta, the Buddha lists things that Sotāpanna cannot do.

550
00:41:12,618 --> 00:41:15,940
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's no mention there that he's not able to break any of the precepts.

551
00:41:16,612 --> 00:41:24,273
[SPEAKER_01]: What satipaṭṭhāna cannot do, what the person who has prepared the ground and then left the ground, laid down the burden, does not care anymore.

552
00:41:24,994 --> 00:41:31,495
[SPEAKER_01]: He can't kill the Buddha, kill an arahant, violate the bhikkhuni, kill his mother, kill his father.

553
00:41:32,135 --> 00:41:37,616
[SPEAKER_01]: He can't take any determination as permanent, any determination as not suffering.

554
00:41:37,676 --> 00:41:40,116
[SPEAKER_01]: He can't take any arisen dhamma as himself.

555
00:41:41,137 --> 00:41:42,457
[SPEAKER_01]: He can't take anybody else

556
00:41:43,450 --> 00:41:44,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are the things that he can't do.

557
00:41:44,771 --> 00:41:45,231
[SPEAKER_01]: He can break it.

558
00:41:45,271 --> 00:41:45,611
[SPEAKER_01]: He can lie.

559
00:41:45,631 --> 00:41:46,111
[SPEAKER_01]: He can cheat.

560
00:41:46,132 --> 00:41:46,492
[SPEAKER_01]: He can steal.

561
00:41:46,552 --> 00:41:48,273
[SPEAKER_01]: He can break all the precepts.

562
00:42:13,437 --> 00:42:18,598
[SPEAKER_01]: I suppose, even if he's pushed in some, like, extreme circumstances, he might be able to kill as well.

563
00:42:19,478 --> 00:42:33,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Not that he wouldn't reap the vipakas, the attractions, but it wouldn't be, as the Buddha said, it wouldn't be as bad as the other forms.

564
00:42:33,901 --> 00:42:40,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, when you have a little cup of water and you drop one drop of dye, everything is dark, black.

565
00:42:40,342 --> 00:42:41,062
[SPEAKER_01]: You just put black.

566
00:42:41,618 --> 00:42:45,440
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, if you have a hundred liters of water, one drop of dye won't make a difference.

567
00:42:45,780 --> 00:42:53,905
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still a drop of dye, a stain, impurity, but because things have changed, that's why he can't be lower than a human.

568
00:42:54,305 --> 00:42:56,086
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't say what kind of human.

569
00:42:56,506 --> 00:43:05,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if he has been a very misbehaving sodhbhāna, most likely he's going to still be a human, but probably in a very, very bad, bad state, bad position, bad so on.

570
00:43:06,231 --> 00:43:09,993
[SPEAKER_01]: But the thing is, again, somebody with the right view to go to such an extent,

571
00:43:10,477 --> 00:43:21,525
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a fear is, if you say that it's not mine, or you have to realize that you become a salesman, but then you have a responsibility to another person, say a child, whatever person,

572
00:43:40,349 --> 00:43:50,392
[SPEAKER_00]: And you fear that if you lose that sense of responsibility, that this... that child will suffer on account of that, because you just... you won't feel it anymore.

573
00:43:51,412 --> 00:43:55,733
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's a fear of losing all sense... sense of shame completely.

574
00:43:56,733 --> 00:44:02,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's exactly why these things are a burden, are a yoke.

575
00:44:09,377 --> 00:44:09,737
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why

576
00:44:10,303 --> 00:44:14,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Family, friends, duties, responsibilities, they're like pies.

577
00:44:15,287 --> 00:44:15,947
[SPEAKER_01]: They pile you.

578
00:44:17,188 --> 00:44:19,670
[SPEAKER_01]: They obstruct you for their reason, because it's not easy.

579
00:44:20,310 --> 00:44:20,491
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

580
00:44:20,551 --> 00:44:26,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, you need to renounce your own child, your own children, their welfare, their anything.

581
00:44:26,895 --> 00:44:32,860
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to leave the whole thing, yet your success is not guaranteed by doing that, because that's only preparation.

582
00:44:33,080 --> 00:44:34,361
[SPEAKER_00]: That's only setting up the ground.

583
00:44:37,783 --> 00:44:39,705
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no guarantee you'll succeed, yet

584
00:44:40,357 --> 00:44:46,998
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were to succeed, you do have to leave it completely, utterly, 100% beforehand.

585
00:44:58,301 --> 00:44:58,641
[SPEAKER_01]: You're right.

586
00:44:58,681 --> 00:45:02,942
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why they wouldn't, you know, they're like not going, no, no, no.

587
00:45:02,982 --> 00:45:09,843
[SPEAKER_01]: They refused in the beginning to teach the householders, because they would understand that, and they would take it on,

588
00:45:10,546 --> 00:45:12,467
[SPEAKER_01]: As, you know, obviously a worth thing to do.

589
00:45:12,987 --> 00:45:14,288
[SPEAKER_01]: And they will leave, the families.

590
00:45:14,488 --> 00:45:15,809
[SPEAKER_01]: They will then go through it and die.

591
00:45:15,869 --> 00:45:18,631
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, no man in the house and that's it.

592
00:45:18,791 --> 00:45:19,231
[SPEAKER_01]: They're done.

593
00:45:20,612 --> 00:45:22,313
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it would affect lives of many people.

594
00:45:22,773 --> 00:45:23,974
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they say, okay, well, don't teach it.

595
00:45:24,014 --> 00:45:27,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Let them... Like, if they find out and they don't find, but don't be directly responsible for that.

596
00:45:29,297 --> 00:45:33,379
[SPEAKER_01]: Just because it will affect other aspects of the order and people and hating and whatever and so on.

597
00:45:33,880 --> 00:45:35,601
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the long run, it's better.

598
00:45:38,322 --> 00:45:39,883
[SPEAKER_01]: But still, like, that's just like, you know,

599
00:45:41,083 --> 00:45:47,026
[SPEAKER_01]: To become an Arahant or something, but there are many Sotāpannas back in the Buddha's day, they were just lay householders.

600
00:45:47,086 --> 00:45:57,171
[SPEAKER_01]: And when the Buddha's father came to him, all in tears, upset, distraught, because the Buddha took his grandson, or the Buddha's son, Rahula.

601
00:45:58,892 --> 00:46:05,775
[SPEAKER_01]: So, imagine his state of mind, and then the Buddha calmed him down, and then gave the teaching, and then he understood, and he became a Sotāpanna right there and then.

602
00:46:07,696 --> 00:46:09,017
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he said, okay, well, thank you.

603
00:46:09,670 --> 00:46:13,413
[SPEAKER_01]: I do have to go because, you know, we assholes are very busy and so on, so I have things to do.

604
00:46:14,774 --> 00:46:16,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Sotāpanna said that upon becoming Sotāpanna.

605
00:46:17,717 --> 00:46:18,778
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not that big a deal.

606
00:46:19,578 --> 00:46:20,439
[SPEAKER_01]: But in a sense, it is.

607
00:46:21,600 --> 00:46:22,701
[SPEAKER_01]: But in another sense, it isn't.

608
00:46:24,122 --> 00:46:25,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's still a lot to be done.

609
00:46:30,627 --> 00:46:34,390
[SPEAKER_00]: How can we see the dhamma by contemplating designation?

610
00:46:36,798 --> 00:46:38,459
[SPEAKER_00]: What is another word for designation?

611
00:46:38,599 --> 00:46:58,311
[SPEAKER_01]: By contemplating that designation is not a problem to begin with, you will learn to allow those more ambiguous, less palpable phenomena, so to speak, such as designations and terms and signs and tokens, to exist, to arise.

612
00:46:59,932 --> 00:47:02,154
[SPEAKER_01]: By not allowing them, you don't see them.

613
00:47:02,354 --> 00:47:04,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, by thinking they are the problem,

614
00:47:05,314 --> 00:47:12,401
[SPEAKER_01]: By denying them, by blaming them, by undoing them, by whatever else people would do, means you wouldn't see them in their nature.

615
00:47:14,103 --> 00:47:15,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you're not seeing the nature of phenomena.

616
00:47:17,266 --> 00:47:20,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you're not seeing the nature of arising.

617
00:47:22,071 --> 00:47:29,298
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could see the dhamma by learning how not to interfere with the designations and what they designate.

618
00:47:30,373 --> 00:47:41,959
[SPEAKER_01]: By learning how not to main... well, by not maintaining the blame on them for your suffering, but by learning how to leave it as it is, then eventually you'll see where the suffering does come from, which is not them.

619
00:47:42,539 --> 00:47:49,943
[SPEAKER_01]: For as long as you think they're the problem, you'll be trying to fix them, which means you'll be misdirecting your efforts externally, outwardly.

620
00:47:51,824 --> 00:47:58,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Not recognizing that the reason for your suffering, as the sister Vajradāna said,

621
00:47:58,504 --> 00:48:01,066
[SPEAKER_01]: It's merely the suffering arising, suffering standing.

622
00:48:02,827 --> 00:48:03,988
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the problem is.

623
00:48:06,790 --> 00:48:21,019
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't have to worry about designations as long as you're not spending your time blaming them for your suffering and saying, well, I'm not real, I don't exist anyway.

624
00:48:21,079 --> 00:48:26,243
[SPEAKER_01]: My name, my ethnicity, my nationality, that's all just

625
00:48:27,500 --> 00:48:28,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Empty convention.

626
00:48:28,701 --> 00:48:29,561
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not real.

627
00:48:30,222 --> 00:48:33,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, when I experience pain, that's not real either.

628
00:48:35,024 --> 00:48:36,705
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a completely gratuitous connection.

629
00:48:37,045 --> 00:48:37,686
[SPEAKER_01]: And it doesn't work.

630
00:48:38,786 --> 00:48:40,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why it doesn't free you from suffering.

631
00:48:42,689 --> 00:48:43,269
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's how.

632
00:48:46,251 --> 00:48:49,893
[SPEAKER_00]: The designations cover quite a range of... Nāma.

633
00:48:50,073 --> 00:48:51,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, nāma.

634
00:48:51,353 --> 00:48:55,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, like, there is no just nāma, or there is no just rūpa.

635
00:48:55,616 --> 00:48:55,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

636
00:48:57,148 --> 00:48:58,128
[SPEAKER_01]: So, everything is designated.

637
00:48:58,148 --> 00:48:58,868
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I'm saying.

638
00:48:59,288 --> 00:49:00,289
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I was saying before.

639
00:49:00,309 --> 00:49:04,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Even you saw, like, I'm not calling this and you just called it, like, like that.

640
00:49:05,209 --> 00:49:06,590
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not going to giving any name to this.

641
00:49:06,810 --> 00:49:08,030
[SPEAKER_01]: You just named it as such.

642
00:49:10,830 --> 00:49:14,491
[SPEAKER_01]: So, name, as in name and matter, it's not optional.

643
00:49:16,331 --> 00:49:18,212
[SPEAKER_01]: So, undoing the name, it's ridiculous.

644
00:49:18,492 --> 00:49:21,032
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't undo the name or you can undo the form.

645
00:49:24,173 --> 00:49:25,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not your doing anyway.

646
00:49:26,614 --> 00:49:27,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Designation is not what you're doing.

647
00:49:29,896 --> 00:49:31,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance in regard to it is.

648
00:49:34,478 --> 00:49:37,020
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but to see that nāma, rūpa is not just nāma.

649
00:49:38,100 --> 00:49:39,581
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, there is not just nāma.

650
00:49:40,041 --> 00:49:43,163
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you can discern the aspect of nāma in nāmarūpa.

651
00:49:43,823 --> 00:49:50,947
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you can discern the resistance aspect of rūpa in nāmarūpa, but there is not just nāma, just rūpa.

652
00:49:52,528 --> 00:49:54,769
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and nāmarūpa with another word?

653
00:49:55,683 --> 00:49:56,864
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the all.

654
00:49:57,505 --> 00:49:58,666
[SPEAKER_00]: But it can also be appearance.

655
00:49:59,287 --> 00:50:00,368
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything that appears.

656
00:50:00,708 --> 00:50:01,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

657
00:50:02,150 --> 00:50:06,354
[SPEAKER_00]: But with wrong view, it exists.

658
00:50:06,695 --> 00:50:08,536
[SPEAKER_01]: When it says the all, it means everything.

659
00:50:09,137 --> 00:50:14,302
[SPEAKER_01]: From things that are seemingly more material to, like, you know, vague, ambiguous, abstract thoughts.

660
00:50:14,823 --> 00:50:16,525
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, that's as a designation.

661
00:50:16,705 --> 00:50:17,566
[SPEAKER_01]: It has its matter.

662
00:50:18,148 --> 00:50:20,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is right here and then the presence of that living body or whatever else.

663
00:50:21,490 --> 00:50:24,032
[SPEAKER_01]: The experience as a whole, nāmarūpa, remains.

664
00:50:24,172 --> 00:50:27,895
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't even conceive that not being so.

665
00:50:28,175 --> 00:50:28,455
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

666
00:50:28,695 --> 00:50:30,717
[SPEAKER_00]: Designation is not just a label.

667
00:50:31,337 --> 00:50:32,218
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

668
00:50:32,378 --> 00:50:34,399
[SPEAKER_01]: A label is a designated designation.

669
00:50:35,420 --> 00:50:42,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, you labeled it, like intentionally intended something, decided on top of the orderly inclinations that you had.

670
00:50:42,765 --> 00:50:43,846
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, that's a label.

671
00:50:45,627 --> 00:50:46,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is...

672
00:50:46,227 --> 00:50:46,788
[SPEAKER_01]: I call this...

673
00:50:48,403 --> 00:50:51,924
[SPEAKER_01]: I label it as such and such, in such and such regard.

674
00:50:52,945 --> 00:50:56,746
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's just like a very proliferated designation, you can say.

675
00:50:57,806 --> 00:51:00,027
[SPEAKER_01]: It still shares the same nature of designation.

676
00:51:01,707 --> 00:51:06,829
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you realize the nature of designation, you will realize that labeling was never a problem.

677
00:51:06,949 --> 00:51:07,849
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of inevitable.

678
00:51:08,529 --> 00:51:11,850
[SPEAKER_01]: If you abstain from the label, you label it like that.

679
00:51:13,171 --> 00:51:17,172
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you can't stop choosing, can't stop making decisions.

680
00:51:17,595 --> 00:51:18,115
[SPEAKER_01]: Perceiving.

681
00:51:18,475 --> 00:51:20,596
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't stop perceiving, you can't stop feeling.

682
00:51:21,376 --> 00:51:24,537
[SPEAKER_01]: All you can do is step outside of the whole thing.

683
00:51:24,977 --> 00:51:33,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, not step outside, literally, but surmount the whole thing, so you're not affected by it.

684
00:51:33,240 --> 00:51:43,623
[SPEAKER_01]: And as the Buddha said, even the Dalai Lama uses designations such as I am, I am this, I was like that, but he does not confuse that designation for his sense of self.

685
00:51:43,803 --> 00:51:47,404
[SPEAKER_01]: He does not stand anymore for the wrong view of the self.

686
00:51:49,360 --> 00:51:50,341
[SPEAKER_01]: He has cleared that out.

687
00:51:50,461 --> 00:51:53,923
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now it's a true, just an empty designation, in that sense.

688
00:51:54,444 --> 00:51:56,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Not empty of everything, empty of its reality.

689
00:51:57,245 --> 00:51:58,806
[SPEAKER_01]: It's empty of appropriation.

690
00:52:00,508 --> 00:52:00,648
Yeah.

691
00:52:03,570 --> 00:52:13,316
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when... so, when a... a sotāpanna says, not mine, he doesn't take that knowledge as his?

692
00:52:14,417 --> 00:52:14,637
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

693
00:52:15,838 --> 00:52:18,620
[SPEAKER_00]: But, therefore, an arahant, he doesn't say, not mine.

694
00:52:18,995 --> 00:52:20,476
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't have to say it anymore.

695
00:52:26,319 --> 00:52:29,940
[SPEAKER_01]: But what Kuntījana can't do, he can deny the whole world, say it's not mine.

696
00:52:30,521 --> 00:52:38,524
[SPEAKER_01]: But what he cannot see as not mine, is he's actually denying the whole world.

697
00:52:38,544 --> 00:52:46,288
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he can see himself with not-self, he can see not-self with self, he can see self with self, but he can't see not-self with not-self.

698
00:52:48,082 --> 00:52:49,743
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that one again, which is why it's subtle.

699
00:52:51,023 --> 00:52:51,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

700
00:52:51,203 --> 00:52:51,404
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

701
00:52:51,424 --> 00:52:56,246
[SPEAKER_00]: No matter what arises, it's not appropriate.

702
00:52:56,306 --> 00:52:59,247
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not... it's not his.

703
00:52:59,887 --> 00:53:01,248
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he sees not-self, not-other.

704
00:53:01,268 --> 00:53:02,488
[SPEAKER_00]: He sees that, not-self.

705
00:53:03,229 --> 00:53:08,751
[SPEAKER_01]: And he needs to persist in it, so the things won't even arise anymore with the offer of self.

706
00:53:15,674 --> 00:53:17,115
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the standing of determination.

707
00:53:18,859 --> 00:53:20,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, you can say so, yeah.

708
00:53:48,623 --> 00:53:55,624
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you were... because if you were not able to designate, you'd be dead.

709
00:53:58,325 --> 00:53:59,465
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not nibbāna.

710
00:54:04,606 --> 00:54:16,948
[SPEAKER_01]: And actually, there is that sutta, yeah, when it says, if an infant, if the conscience turns away from the name and form, would

711
00:54:23,611 --> 00:54:26,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Based on the list and designation, I've learned a lot about that.

712
00:54:38,647 --> 00:54:38,887
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

713
00:54:39,407 --> 00:54:41,668
[SPEAKER_01]: With name and matter as condition, consciousness.

714
00:54:42,288 --> 00:54:42,929
[SPEAKER_01]: So it was said.

715
00:54:43,049 --> 00:54:46,470
[SPEAKER_01]: How it is, Ananda, that with name and matter as condition, there is consciousness.

716
00:54:47,070 --> 00:54:48,190
[SPEAKER_01]: It should be seen in this manner.

717
00:54:48,751 --> 00:54:58,474
[SPEAKER_01]: If Ananda consciousness were not to obtain a stay in name and matter, would future arising, coming into a being of birth, aging, death, unpleasure, suffering, be manifest?

718
00:54:58,634 --> 00:54:59,054
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't.

719
00:55:00,195 --> 00:55:06,797
[SPEAKER_01]: So, therefore, that's the reason, that's the occasion, that it's the arising, that this is the condition of consciousness, that's to stay in name and matter.

720
00:55:07,378 --> 00:55:31,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus far and under may one be born, or age, or die, or fall, or arise, thus far is there a way of designation, as far as designations can go, thus far there is a way of language, thus far there is a way of description, thus far there is a sphere of understanding, thus far the round proceeds as manifestation in a situation, so far, that is to say, as there is name and matter together with consciousness.

721
00:55:37,632 --> 00:55:41,235
[SPEAKER_00]: And for other realms, like, yeah, the same thing.

722
00:55:41,495 --> 00:55:47,340
[SPEAKER_01]: They designate, but they might... Yeah, well, the faculties are different, more limited, and so on.

723
00:55:47,420 --> 00:55:49,461
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they wouldn't have...

724
00:55:49,481 --> 00:55:50,822
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't have the conventional means.

725
00:55:50,842 --> 00:55:52,424
[SPEAKER_01]: The degree of awareness, yeah.

726
00:55:52,764 --> 00:55:58,728
[SPEAKER_01]: But animal, like, they might not be able to speak, but they have that which stands in the form of speech.

727
00:55:59,329 --> 00:56:02,872
[SPEAKER_01]: The sounds they make, the expressions, and it's a form of communication.

728
00:56:02,892 --> 00:56:04,513
[SPEAKER_01]: It's much more, obviously, cruder,

729
00:56:05,353 --> 00:56:15,141
[SPEAKER_01]: The five aggregates are the five aggregates in any realm, be it the deva realm, or the animal realm, or the hell realm.

730
00:56:16,422 --> 00:56:22,647
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no absence of like, oh, I have only two aggregates, or something like that.

731
00:56:22,687 --> 00:56:24,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Just five aggregates, the nature of things.

732
00:56:27,319 --> 00:56:35,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the composition, the ways of going about, might be different for an animal than a human, which is why animal realm is more unfortunate than human.

733
00:56:37,084 --> 00:56:38,464
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's still the same.

734
00:56:38,565 --> 00:56:42,306
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's such a perilous realm to be in, animal realm.

735
00:56:43,187 --> 00:56:48,830
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have awareness of your choice, yet you are choosing, and you're fully responsible for the results of that choice.

736
00:56:49,670 --> 00:56:50,991
[SPEAKER_01]: So an animal can't help.

737
00:56:51,751 --> 00:56:54,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Dog can't help, but chase after things, or hunt things.

738
00:56:54,412 --> 00:56:55,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Cat can't help, but kill it.

739
00:56:57,441 --> 00:56:59,121
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's still responsible for it.

740
00:56:59,762 --> 00:57:00,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean, oh, it can't help.

741
00:57:01,062 --> 00:57:01,842
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not her fault.

742
00:57:01,882 --> 00:57:03,843
[SPEAKER_01]: It is her fault.

743
00:57:03,863 --> 00:57:04,703
[SPEAKER_01]: The fire is there.

744
00:57:04,783 --> 00:57:05,663
[SPEAKER_01]: It is a form of choice.

745
00:57:08,084 --> 00:57:12,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, there is not enough awareness for that choice to be recognized.

746
00:57:12,064 --> 00:57:22,187
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they just go with the most obvious inclination that they would have, which is why it's so hard to step outside of the animal realm, because you're just perpetuating the bad action.

747
00:57:22,867 --> 00:57:26,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Action, action fully immersed in greed, aversion, delusion.

748
00:57:27,512 --> 00:57:29,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Completely.

749
00:57:29,394 --> 00:57:41,004
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you would really need an external circumstances, stroke of luck, to get back from an animal realm, for which you're responsible going to, in the first place, through your bad actions, in other better realms.

750
00:57:42,205 --> 00:57:54,055
[SPEAKER_01]: And that stroke of luck is, basically, that that simile of the blind turtle coming up at once 500 years, once every 500 years, to the top of the sea, just for a second, and sticking its head

751
00:57:54,715 --> 00:57:58,438
[SPEAKER_01]: to a yoke that's been floating around and thrown around by winds and waves.

752
00:57:58,458 --> 00:57:59,658
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, what are the chances?

753
00:58:00,379 --> 00:58:06,383
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, those are the chances of somebody from an animal realm to come back.

754
00:58:06,403 --> 00:58:19,972
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, like, there is, there is some, like, looking after an animal, helping an animal, feeding it, does, in a sense, like, by making an animal restrain itself,

755
00:58:21,423 --> 00:58:29,648
[SPEAKER_01]: A natural restraint is there in its behavior, and as such, it will count, bit by bit, eventually, in the future.

756
00:58:31,509 --> 00:58:41,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, they don't know... they're not... they're not... they didn't willingly choose that restraint by the fact that restraint was imposed upon them, in a sense of wait before you jump on your foot.

757
00:58:42,955 --> 00:58:47,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Means there is a phenomenon of restraint present in their experience right there, right then.

758
00:58:48,778 --> 00:58:50,319
[SPEAKER_01]: A wholesome phenomenon present.

759
00:58:51,556 --> 00:59:01,360
[SPEAKER_01]: And Buddha said, in these unwholesome realms, like completely always in darkness, even a tiny little spark of light can mean a lot.

760
00:59:16,647 --> 00:59:17,748
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I look after the dog.

761
00:59:19,292 --> 00:59:21,632
[SPEAKER_01]: And cats, and birds, and monkeys...

